      <h2>How to Verify a VPS Deal Is Still Working (Before You Click)</h2>
      <p>VPS deals decay fast. A "76% off" banner that's live today can quietly change to "50% off" tomorrow, and the only way to know is to verify yourself. Here's the 3-minute recipe I use before publishing any deal in this article:</p>
      <ol>
        <li><strong>Open the deal URL in an incognito window.</strong> This prevents your browser cookies and prior visits from biasing the displayed price.</li>
        <li><strong>Look for the discount in the cart — not just the landing page.</strong> Many providers show a teaser price on the landing page but the actual cart total differs. Click "Order Now" or "Add to Cart" before trusting the price.</li>
        <li><strong>Check the renewal rate tooltip.</strong> Most intro discounts renew at 2-4x the first-term price. Hover over the renewal date or click the renewal info icon to see the post-promo rate.</li>
        <li><strong>Search LowEndBox or LowEndTalk for recent complaints.</strong> The <a href="https://lowendbox.com/" target="_blank" rel="noopener">LowEndBox forum</a> and its sibling <a href="https://lowendtalk.com/" target="_blank" rel="noopener">LowEndTalk</a> are where hosting providers' actual customers report deal changes, billing surprises, and support quality drops.</li>
        <li><strong>Check the "fine print" footer of the cart.</strong> Some deals exclude setup fees, IPv4 charges, or backups. Read the cart summary line by line before paying.</li>
      </ol>
      <p>Why this matters: vpstier affiliate links (including <a href="https://my.racknerd.com/aff.php?aff=16299" target="_blank" rel="noopener">the RackNerd link used throughout this article</a>) carry a 90-day cookie. If you sign up via the link and the deal turns out to be materially different than described, you can request a refund within the provider's money-back window — but it's much better to verify in advance.</p>
